  <div class="story_text">

  <p>
  Hackt News is the community news aggregator for <strong><a href="https://hackt.org">HACKT</a></strong>, the reopening of
    <a href="https://freaknet.org">FreakNet Medialab</a> - Italy's first hacklab, founded in Catania in 1995.
  </p>

  <p>
    We're a space for hackers, makers, and digital culture enthusiasts to share and discuss
    stories about technology, security, programming, art, and the intersection of tech and society.
    Born from the legacy of Italian underground hacker culture, HACKT carries forward
    FreakNet's spirit of experimentation, knowledge sharing, and digital freedom.
  </p>


  <h2 id="guidelines">Guidelines</h2>

  <div id="topicality">
    <p>
    <strong>Topicality</strong>:
    Hackt News focuses on computing, hacking, and digital culture. Great stories to submit:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Technical deep dives and tutorials</li>
      <li>Security research and discoveries</li>
      <li>Programming projects and tools</li>
      <li>Digital rights and privacy</li>
      <li>Retrocomputing and hardware hacking</li>
      <li>Net art and digital culture</li>
      <li>Stories about hacker/maker communities</li>
    </ul>

  </div>

  <h2 id="tagging">Tags</h2>
  <p>
  When links or stories are submitted, they must be tagged by the submitter from a list of predefined <a href="/tags">tags</a>.
  Users can choose to <a href="/filters">filter</a> out or
  subscribe to all submissions with particular tags (example: <a href="https://hackt.news/t/programming.rss">programming.rss</a>)
  or combinations of tags (example: <a href="https://hackt.news/t/programming,security.rss">programming,security.rss</a>).
  All users see all stories by default.
  </p>


  <h2 id="invitations">Invitation Tree</h2>
  <p>
  The full <%= link_to 'user tree', users_tree_path %> is public and each user's profile
  shows who invited them. This provides some degree of accountability and
  helps identify voting rings.
  </p>

  <p>
  Invitations are used as a mechanism for spam-control and to encourage users to be nice.
  Users are considered "new" for their first <%= User::NEW_USER_DAYS %> days,
  and their usernames appear <span class="new_user">in green</span>.
  </p>

  <p>
  The quickest way to receive an invitation is to talk to someone you <%= link_to 'recognize from the site', users_tree_path %>.
  <% if Rails.application.allow_invitation_requests? %>
    You can also <a href="/invitations/request">request an invite</a>.
  <% end %>
  </p>


  <h2 id="ranking">Ranking</h2>
  <p>
  All story and comment ranking on this site comes from user activity.
  All users have equal votes, with no special priorities or penalties for specific users or domains.
  Moderators have no ability to manipulate rankings besides voting like any user.
  </p>

  <div id="flags">
    <p>
    Users can flag stories and comments when there's a serious problem that needs moderator attention.
    Users must reach <%= User::MIN_KARMA_TO_FLAG %> karma to flag.
    Flagging requires selecting from a preset list of reasons to guide usage.
    </p>
  </div>




  <h2 id="transparency">Transparency Policy</h2>

  <p>
    Even though this place is quite unmoderated, you still have <a href="/moderators">moderators</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
  All <a href="/moderations">moderator actions</a> on this site are visible to
  everyone and the identities of those moderators are <%= link_to 'public', moderators_path %>.
  While the individual actions of a moderator may cause debate, there should be no question about if an action happened or who is responsible.
  </p>

  <p>
  If users are disruptive enough to warrant banning, they will be banned
  absolutely, given notice of their banning, and their disabled user profile
  will indicate which moderator banned them and why.
  There will be no <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_banning">shadow banning</a> or other secret moderation actions.
  </p>


  <h2 id="features">Technical Features</h2>

  <ul>
  <li><p>
  <strong>Private messaging</strong> enables users to communicate privately
  without having to publicly disclose an e-mail address.
  </p></li>

  <li><p>
  <strong>Responsive design</strong> works great on phones and tablets.
  </p></li>

  <li><p>
  <strong><a href="/search">Integrated search engine</a></strong> covers all
  submitted stories and comments, including full-text caches of all submitted
  story contents.
  </p></li>

  <li id="story-hiding"><p>
    <strong>Story hiding</strong> to remove individual stories from your view.
    Hidden stories won't appear on the homepage, tag pages, or in searches.
    Users can unhide stories from their <a href="/hidden">list of hidden stories</a>.
  </p></li>

  <li><p>
  <strong><a href="/hats">Hats</a></strong> allow users to post comments while "wearing a hat" to
  give their words more authority (such as an employee speaking for the company).
  </p></li>

  <li><p>
  <strong><a href="/rss">Per-tag, multi-tag and site-wide RSS feeds</a></strong> are
  available to the public and logged-in users have private RSS feeds that
  filter out each user's <a href="/filters">filtered tags</a>.
  </p></li>
  </ul>


